WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.808
Here is a payment class with six fields, in Java and in Kotlin.

2
00:00:06.808 --> 00:00:09.220
How many bytes does one Payment take?

3
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:18.400
JOL, OpenJDK's object layout tool, prints the real answer on JDK 21: 40 bytes, for both.

4
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:21.559
Every object starts with a header.

5
00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:23.269
The mark word is 8 bytes.

6
00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:29.152
It holds the identity hash code, the GC age, and the lock bits that synchronized uses.

7
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.680
Then a class pointer, compressed to 4 bytes, says which class this is.

8
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.171
That's a 12-byte header.

9
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.835
The fields come next, but not in the order you wrote them.

10
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:44.296
The long can't start at 12, because a long must start at a multiple of 8.

11
00:00:44.296 --> 00:00:48.595
So it goes to 16, and the JVM puts the int in the gap at 12.

12
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:51.598
The boolean and the byte take one byte each.

13
00:00:51.598 --> 00:00:55.009
After a 2-byte gap come the references, 4 bytes each.

14
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.409
That's 36 bytes.

15
00:00:56.409 --> 00:01:01.016
Objects start at multiples of 8, so the size rounds up to 40.

16
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:05.330
The rule: header, plus the fields, rounded up to 8.

17
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.543
12 plus 22 is 34, which rounds up to 40.

18
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:12.425
A reference on a 64-bit machine should take 8 bytes.

19
00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:18.603
Here each one takes 4, thanks to compressed oops: an oop is an ordinary object pointer.

20
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.267
Every object starts at a multiple of 8, so the last 3 bits of its address are always zero.

21
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.076
So the JVM drops them.

22
00:01:27.076 --> 00:01:33.236
It stores a 32-bit number, and shifts it left by 3 bits when it follows the reference.

23
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:38.005
2 to the 32 slots, times 8 bytes, is 32 GB.

24
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:45.234
Give the JVM a heap of 32 GB or more, and it turns compressed oops off.

25
00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:48.863
References grow to 8 bytes, and Payment to 48.

26
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.988
An ArrayList of 1,000 Integers grows by 24%.

27
00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:57.891
So a 40 GB heap holds less data than its size suggests.

28
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:08.724
On JDK 25, the flag UseCompactObjectHeaders moves the class pointer into the mark word, so the header is 8 bytes.

29
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:11.684
JOL's simulation puts Payment at 32.

30
00:02:11.684 --> 00:02:16.063
Each object saves 8 bytes, or nothing, because of padding.
